# SVN Create Review

Prepare a complete review package without pretending SVN has pull requests.

## Establish the source state

1. Record the SVN client version, working-copy root, repository root and UUID, URL, and
   working revision state.
2. Detect mixed revisions, switched or sparse paths, conflicts, missing or obstructed
   items, locks, and modified externals. Treat a review branch as repository convention,
   not a special SVN object.
3. Inspect `svn status --no-ignore`. When repository access is authorized, use
   `svn status -u` to detect out-of-date items; plain `svn status` is local-only.
4. Run `svn diff --summarize` and a scoped `svn diff`. State that ordinary working-copy
   diffs compare against `BASE` and omit unversioned or ignored file contents. Inventory
   those paths separately and identify intended additions.
5. Read history with an explicit target, revision range, and limit. Do not present a
   local-path log ending at an old working revision as current repository history.

## Prepare the review

Return a review title and description containing the purpose, exact source state,
repository-relative changed paths, property and external changes, patch coverage
limits, tests run and not run, risks, and reviewer notes. Redact credentials and
sensitive data from diffs and excerpts.

If the user requests a mutating next step, show one exact proposed command separately
and explain its effect. A working-copy-to-working-copy copy schedules an addition; a
working-copy-to-URL or URL-to-URL copy creates a repository commit immediately.

Never create a patch file, commit, copy remotely, upload, email, or change an external
review system without explicit confirmation. If an approved remote operation runs,
return its committed revision and verify the target URL and changed paths read-only.
